<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <!--
      Base application theme.

      Dark, not `DayNight` (ADR-0054). The shell renders a site that is dark
      unconditionally — `app/global.css` declares its tokens once in `:root`
      rather than under a `.dark` class, and `next` never emits a light one — and
      the window behind it is #0f172a (`capacitor.config.ts`). A DayNight parent
      therefore has exactly one visible effect: on a phone in light mode every
      surface the *shell* owns, rather than the site, renders light and lands on
      top of a dark product. That is what the platform-styled dialog did before
      this, and it was the loudest thing wrong with it.

      `colorPrimary`/`colorPrimaryDark`/`colorAccent` used to point at
      `@color/colorPrimary` and friends, which this repository has never defined
      — they resolved out of a dependency that happens to ship those names, which
      is why nothing failed loudly. They now name the brand.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat">
        <item name="colorPrimary">@color/roy_primary</item>
        <item name="colorPrimaryDark">@color/roy_background</item>
        <item name="colorAccent">@color/roy_primary</item>
    </style>

    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.AppCompat.NoActionBar">
        <item name="windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="windowNoTitle">true</item>
        <item name="android:background">@null</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The launch theme is the whole splash: one theme, read by the platform on
      Android 12+ and by `androidx.core:core-splashscreen` below it (which the
      launcher, `ShellActivity`, installs), so every Android version draws the same
      splash from the same three items and no splash-specific drawable or bitmap
      exists.

      `windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon` is `@drawable/splash_icon` — generated from
      the same artwork as the launcher icon, with its disc at
      `TARGET_DISC_RATIO / LAUNCHER_MAGNIFICATION` of the canvas. Both splash
      implementations mask the icon down to a circle two thirds of the area they
      draw it in, which is exactly the launcher's own magnification, so the disc
      lands on the launcher icon's 64% rather than on the artwork's own share; the
      numbers are in `docs/mobile/android-shell.md`.

      The background is `@color/roy_background`, the generated token the shell's
      first Compose frame paints (`ShellFrame`), so the splash lifts straight onto
      the app's own frame with no colour change between the two. It is deliberately
      not `@color/roy_launch_background`: that is the document's surface, which a
      destination is handed over to after the frame (#647, ADR-0105).
      `postSplashScreenTheme` is the theme the activity switches to once the splash
      is dismissed. Nothing is held here: the platform dismisses the splash on the
      app's first drawn frame — the shell's own — and no duration is declared, so
      there is no clock to keep in step with the document.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBarLaunch" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/roy_background</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_icon</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme.NoActionBar</item>
    </style>
</resources>
